, and the record is 71\u00b0F (1999). The low of <strong>24\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Record highs keep coming. Record lows have nearly stopped.","p_rec":"If Minneapolis' climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Minneapolis has set <strong class=\"sig\">6 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 64 that chance alone would give, and <strong class=\"sig\">114 record highs</strong>, far more than the 64 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (57). Minneapolis' all-time high is 108\u00b0F, on July 14, 1936.","t_chg":"Minneapolis has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 153 complete years: Minneapolis' average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.24\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.17 to +0.31), about +3.6\u00b0F since 1873. Minneapolis' average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.18\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.12 to +0.25), about +2.8\u00b0F since 1873.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 9 of 12 months. Highs rose in 8 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Minneapolis has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Minneapolis' yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.24 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.05 to +0.44), about +3.8 inches since 1871. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 31.6 inches a year. The wettest year was 2019 (43.17 in); the driest, 1910 (11.54 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 5 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}